DiLorenzo", "Prediction markets price Shaheen around 93-94%, and most expert ratings land at Likely D", "The only poll, a UNH survey of likely voters, shows a narrower D+4 race"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a clear but not overwhelming Democratic edge in this open, slightly blue-leaning district, and markets and most raters agree. The single recent poll at D+4 and a contested, well-funded Republican nominee argue for trimming the expected margin slightly below the consensus-implied number.", "watch": "If DiLorenzo's wealth-and-disclosures attacks fade while his fundraising sustains heavy advertising, or if the national environment shifts further toward Republicans, this could tighten toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chris Pappas (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Hampshire's 1st District is an open seat after Chris Pappas left to run for Senate, with Democrat Stefany Shaheen facing Republican Anthony DiLorenzo. The district leans Democratic by only about two points, so the seat is competitive on paper. Shaheen enters the general with a strong financial position, holding more cash on hand and benefiting from roughly $2.2 million in outside support, while DiLorenzo has raised comparably but spent heavily in the primary and has little cash remaining.\n\nA late-September University of New Hampshire poll of likely voters showed Shaheen ahead by four points, narrower than expert ratings imply. Most analysts rate the race Likely Democratic, with a few at Lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Shaheen roughly a 93 percent chance. DiLorenzo has faced questions about his financial disclosures, while Shaheen has been attacked as an establishment favorite. The race leans clearly Democratic but is not out of reach for Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.937, "margin": 10.01}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9238, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9245, "margin": 9.5}, {"date